
(verb :name "read-error?" :std "R7RS-small" :library "exception" :tier "core" :kind "predicate" :signature "(read-error? obj) -> boolean"
  :summary "R7RS §6.11 — #t when obj is a condition raised by `read` because the text would not parse."
  :explanation "The one predicate that separates 'your input is malformed' from 'your program is wrong', and the two want opposite reactions. When `read` meets text it cannot finish — an unclosed list, an unterminated string, a stray close paren — it raises a condition that read-error? answers #t for, and the message names the fault and the line and column it stopped at. Every other raised value answers #f: a condition from `error`, a condition from `escalate`, an engine fault, a bare symbol somebody raised, and any value that was never raised at all. So a handler can ask read-error? first and take the input-was-bad branch, and fall through to the something-is-broken branch otherwise. That distinction is what lets a cart accept text a person typed without treating every typo as a crash."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(read-error? 'anything)" :note "#f — an ordinary value is not a read condition; the predicate is safe to ask about anything.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (read-error? e)) (lambda () (read (open-input-string \"(1 2\"))))" :note "#t — the list was never closed, so read refused and the condition is a read error.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (read-error? e)) (lambda () (error \"not a read problem\")))" :note "#f — a condition from error is not a read error, which is exactly the branch this predicate exists to separate.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (list (read-error? e) (error-object-message e))) (lambda () (read (open-input-string \"\\\"abc\"))))" :note "(#t \"unterminated string (line 1, col 1)\") — the message names the fault and where the reader gave up.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(define (safe-read text)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (if (read-error? e) (list 'bad-syntax (error-object-message e)) (list 'other-failure)))\n    (lambda () (read (open-input-string text)))))\n(display (list (safe-read \"(a b)\") (safe-read \"(a b\")))\n(newline)" :note "((a b) (bad-syntax missing ) (line 1, col 1))) — good text parses, bad text is reported as syntax rather than as a crash."))
  :caveats ("End of input is NOT a read error. (read p) on an exhausted port returns the eof object; test it with eof-object?, not with a handler." "A condition from error, from escalate, or from an engine fault answers #f. Only the reader raises read conditions." "The predicate answers #f for any non-condition, so a #f tells you nothing on its own about whether anything was raised." "Motoi has no file ports, so the only way to reach a read error is a string port from open-input-string.")
  :usecases ("Separating malformed user input from a bug in the program that is reading it." "Reporting the line and column of a syntax fault back to whoever typed it." "Falling back to a default datum when a saved cart or config will not parse." "Stopping a read-every-datum loop cleanly at the first unparseable form.")
  :related ("read" "error-object-message" "file-error?" "with-exception-handler" "eof-object?" "open-input-string")
  :learn (:concept "Not all failures deserve the same reaction: bad input is the user's to fix, a bug is yours, and a predicate on the condition is how the handler tells them apart." :prerequisites ("read" "with-exception-handler" "open-input-string") :progression "Read a good datum first, then a malformed one inside a handler, then branch on read-error? so the two paths report differently.")
  :provenance "R7RS §6.11; implementation lib/base/r7rs-small.js:972; measured against the live engine 2026-08-02; reference record added 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (safe-read text)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (if (read-error? e) (list 'bad-syntax (error-object-message e)) (list 'other-failure)))\n    (lambda () (read (open-input-string text)))))\n(display (list (safe-read \"(a b)\") (safe-read \"(a b\")))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (read-error? e)) (lambda () (read (open-input-string \"(1 (2 3)\")))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list (read-error? 'a) (read-error? 42) (read-error? \"s\")))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A cart loads a saved level that a person may have hand-edited. Candidates: count the parens yourself before reading (a partial reimplementation of the reader that will disagree with it on strings and comments), catch everything and always report 'bad file' (which hides a genuine bug in the loader behind a message blaming the user), or catch and ask read-error?."
     :explanation "eng: two report shapes from one wrapper, chosen by the predicate. math: a nested list missing one closer is still a read error — the reader tracks depth, so you do not have to. cs: the predicate is total and answers #f for ordinary values, so asking is never itself a risk."
     :meta "Reach for read-error? and NOT a paren counter. The reader already knows the grammar, including that a paren inside a string is not a paren; any counter you write is a second, worse copy of that knowledge that will drift from the first.")
    (:eng "The operator gets 'line 1, col 1' rather than 'load failed', which is the difference between a fixable report and a shrug." :math "Bracket matching is a depth counter over the token stream, and the reader is where that counter lives." :cs "Distinguishing user error from program error at the catch site is the whole reason typed conditions exist."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Ask the failure which kind it is, so the two kinds can be answered differently."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "exception" :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (read-error? e)) (lambda () (read (open-input-string \"(1 2\"))))")
       (:domain "a form rejected at the counter versus a broken till" :code "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (list 'read? (read-error? e) 'file? (file-error? e))) (lambda () (read (open-input-string \"(oops\")))))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "a proof that fails on a malformed statement versus on a false one" :code "(display (map (lambda (t) (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (read-error? e)) t)) (list (lambda () (read (open-input-string \"(1\"))) (lambda () (error \"logic bug\")))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "A clerk hands a form back because a box is blank; the till breaking is not the customer's problem and the customer is not asked to fix it. A statement that is not well-formed is rejected before anyone asks whether it is true. A read error is the blank box: the text never became a datum, so nothing downstream is at fault yet."
     :meta "Well-formedness comes before meaning, in every one of these. Sorting failures by which stage they happened in is what tells you who has to act — and a system that collapses the stages tells everybody the same unhelpful thing.")
    (:eng "Handing the fault back to the layer that can fix it is the whole job of classification." :math "Syntax is checked before semantics; a malformed formula has no truth value to be wrong about." :cs "Parse errors versus runtime errors — the same split every compiler makes, available here at the value level."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (read-error? e)) (lambda () (read (open-input-string \"(1 2\")))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (read-error? e)) (lambda () (error \"not a read problem\"))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list (read-error? #t) (read-error? '()) (read-error? (list 1))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "read-error? is #t for a condition raised by read on malformed text and #f for everything else, including conditions from error and values nothing raised."
     :invariant "For all values v. read-error?(v) = #t if and only if v is the condition raised by read on text it could not parse"
     :meta "Three programs, three sides of the same claim: a real read fault answers #t, a genuine but unrelated condition answers #f, and ordinary values answer #f without raising. What would falsify it: a #t from the second program, which would mean the predicate is really asking 'was anything raised at all' — and would make every handler that branches on it misroute every error."
    )
    (:eng "The #f from the error case is the load-bearing test; without it the predicate would be a rubber stamp." :math "An if-and-only-if, so both directions have to be shown — most predicates are only ever tested in one." :cs "Total on all values: asking about a non-condition is safe and answers #f."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (parse-or-default text fallback)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (if (read-error? e) fallback (raise e))) (lambda () (read (open-input-string text)))))\n(display (list (parse-or-default \"(1 2 3)\" 'default) (parse-or-default \"(1 2\" 'default)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (read-all text)\n  (let ((p (open-input-string text)))\n    (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (list 'stopped (read-error? e)))\n      (lambda () (let loop ((acc '())) (let ((d (read p))) (if (eof-object? d) (reverse acc) (loop (cons d acc)))))))))\n(display (list (read-all \"1 2 3\") (read-all \"1 (2\")))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (lint text)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (if (read-error? e) (list 'syntax (error-object-message e)) (list 'runtime))) (lambda () (begin (read (open-input-string text)) (list 'ok)))))\n(display (list (lint \"(define x 1)\") (lint \"(define x\")))\n(newline)"
     :base "read-error? tells a syntax failure apart from every other failure."
     :emergent "eng: parse-or-default — swallow the syntax fault and re-raise anything else, so a bug in the loader still surfaces. math: read-all — a loop that ends on eof and reports separately when it ended on a fault instead. cs: a one-datum linter that answers ok, syntax, or runtime."
     :meta "The move worth copying is in the first composition: `(if (read-error? e) fallback (raise e))`. Recovering from the failures you understand and RE-RAISING the rest is what keeps a recovery from turning into a place bugs go to die. A handler that returns a fallback unconditionally is how a broken loader ships looking fine."
    )
    (:eng "Re-raising the unrecognised condition is the difference between a guard and a gag." :math "The loop's two exits — eof and fault — are different facts and the caller can tell which happened." :cs "ok / syntax / runtime is the smallest useful classification a tool can report."))
)

(verb :name "file-error?" :std "R7RS-small" :library "exception" :tier "core" :kind "predicate" :signature "(file-error? obj) -> boolean"
  :summary "R7RS §6.11 — #t when obj is a condition raised by a file operation. In Motoi it is ALWAYS #f, because Motoi has no file operations."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(file-error? 'anything)" :note "#f — and this is the answer for every argument, because nothing in Motoi raises a file condition.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (file-error? e)) (lambda () (error \"disk full\")))" :note "#f — a message that mentions a disk is still not a file condition; the predicate reads the condition, not the prose.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(map file-error? (list 1 \"s\" 'sym #t '()))" :note "(#f #f #f #f #f) — constant #f across every kind of value.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (list 'read (read-error? e) 'file (file-error? e))) (lambda () (read (open-input-string \"(1\"))))" :note "(read #t file #f) — the two R7RS condition predicates side by side: one of them can be #t here, the other cannot ever be.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(define (classify-failure thunk)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (cond ((read-error? e) 'syntax) ((file-error? e) 'storage) (else 'logic))) thunk))\n(display (list (classify-failure (lambda () (read (open-input-string \"(1\")))) (classify-failure (lambda () (error \"oops\")))))\n(newline)" :note "(syntax logic) — the storage branch is written, portable, and unreachable on this engine."))
  :caveats ("In Motoi this predicate is hardwired to #f. It cannot answer #t for any argument — see lib/base/r7rs-small.js:974." "Motoi has no file ports at all: open-input-file, open-output-file, file-exists? and delete-file are UNBOUND, so trying one raises an unbound-symbol condition, and that condition is not a file error either." "Because there is nothing to open, the constant #f is vacuously honest today. It is not a guarantee about tomorrow: if file ports are ever added, this predicate has to be reimplemented or it will silently misclassify every storage fault." "A message containing the word 'file' or 'disk' has no effect on the answer. The predicate looks at the condition, not the text." "The storage branch of a cond is still worth writing for portable code — it just never fires here.")
  :usecases ("Writing a condition classifier that will still be correct when the same code runs on a Scheme that does have file ports." "Documenting, at the catch site, that storage failures were considered." "Confirming that a failure is NOT a storage failure before blaming the disk.")
  :related ("read-error?" "error-object?" "error-object-message" "with-exception-handler" "open-input-string")
  :learn (:concept "A standard predicate can be present, conformant, and constant, when the feature it asks about does not exist in this implementation." :prerequisites ("with-exception-handler" "read-error?") :progression "See read-error? answer #t first so you know what a live condition predicate looks like, then compare it with file-error? on the same condition and notice the second one cannot move.")
  :provenance "R7RS §6.11; implementation lib/base/r7rs-small.js:974 returns #f unconditionally, per decision-010 (no file ports); measured against the live engine 2026-08-02; reference record added 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (open-safely path)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (if (file-error? e) (list 'file-problem path) (list 'not-a-file-problem (error-object-message e))))\n    (lambda () (open-input-file path))))\n(display (open-safely \"/no/such/file\"))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (map file-error? (list 1 \"s\" 'sym #t '())))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (list 'read (read-error? e) 'file (file-error? e))) (lambda () (read (open-input-string \"(1\")))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "Code ported from another Scheme wraps an open in a handler and branches on file-error?. Candidates: delete the branch (loses the portability and the record of the decision), assume it works (the branch never fires and the failure falls silently into the else), or keep the branch and know what it costs."
     :explanation "eng: the first program is the whole warning — the open fails, and the failure is reported as NOT a file problem, because open-input-file is unbound rather than unopenable. math: the predicate is a constant function on the whole value space. cs: read-error? and file-error? asked of one condition, and only one of them is capable of a #t."
     :meta "Reach for file-error? only as a portability placeholder. If you need to know whether THIS run failed, ask error-object-message or error-object-type; the file predicate cannot distinguish anything on this engine, so a program whose recovery depends on it has no recovery at all here.")
    (:eng "Reporting a missing file as 'not a file problem' is exactly the wrong-way-round answer an operator would be misled by; that is why it is in the record." :math "A constant function carries no information: knowing the output tells you nothing about the input." :cs "A predicate that cannot be #t is a branch the reader believes is live and the machine knows is dead."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "A question that is well formed and always has the same answer, because the thing it asks about is not present."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "exception" :code "(file-error? 'anything)")
       (:domain "a fuel gauge on a machine with no tank" :code "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (file-error? e)) (lambda () (error \"disk full\"))))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "a predicate that is false over an empty domain" :code "(display (list (file-error? (list 'enoent \"/tmp/x\")) (file-error? \"ENOENT\")))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "A gauge wired to a tank that was never fitted reads empty forever, and reading it teaches you nothing about the machine. A predicate quantified over an empty set is vacuously false for every candidate. file-error? is both: the set of file conditions in Motoi is empty, so the honest answer everywhere is #f."
     :meta "Vacuous truth is not a bug and it is not a fact either. The trap is that a vacuous answer LOOKS like a measurement, so a reader takes #f as evidence that this particular failure was not storage-related — when it is really evidence of nothing at all.")
    (:eng "An instrument that cannot move should be labelled, not trusted." :math "Every statement about the members of the empty set holds; none of them is informative." :cs "Dead branch, live-looking code. The comment in the engine is what keeps it honest."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (map file-error? (list 0 -1 \"\" \"ENOENT\" 'file-error #t #f '() (list 1 2))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (file-error? e)) (lambda () (error \"anything at all\" 1 2 3))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (list (error-object? e) (file-error? e))) (lambda () (escalate 'disk-full \"/tmp\"))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "file-error? returns #f for every argument without exception — including a genuine condition object, and including one escalated under the name disk-full."
     :invariant "For all values v. file-error?(v) = #f"
     :meta "The third program is the sharp one: error-object? answers #t on the same value that file-error? answers #f on, so the value really is a condition and really is named disk-full, and the predicate still says no. That is the proof that it reads nothing. What would falsify the invariant: a single argument anywhere that produced #t — which would mean the engine gained file ports and this record needs rewriting."
    )
    (:eng "Nine arguments of nine shapes, one answer. The test is cheap and it is the only test that matters here." :math "Constant function on the universe of values, which is the strongest form the claim can take." :cs "A regression guard: the day this stops being true is the day the record is wrong."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (classify-failure thunk)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (cond ((read-error? e) 'syntax) ((file-error? e) 'storage) (else 'logic))) thunk))\n(display (list (classify-failure (lambda () (read (open-input-string \"(1\")))) (classify-failure (lambda () (error \"oops\")))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(display (list 'always-false (equal? (map file-error? (list 1 2 3)) (list #f #f #f))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (portable-guard thunk)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (if (file-error? e) 'retry-storage 'give-up)) thunk))\n(display (list (portable-guard (lambda () 'fine)) (portable-guard (lambda () (error \"boom\")))))\n(newline)"
     :base "file-error? is a standard condition predicate that is constantly #f on this engine."
     :emergent "eng: a three-way classifier whose middle branch is dead here and live elsewhere. math: the constant-#f property written as a test that a future engine change would break. cs: a portable retry-on-storage guard that, on Motoi, always chooses give-up."
     :meta "What emerges is a portability seam. The same source runs on two engines and means two different things: on a Scheme with file ports the storage branch is a recovery, and on Motoi it is documentation. Both are legitimate — as long as the record says which one you are getting, which is the whole reason this verb has a caveats list longer than its summary."
    )
    (:eng "Write the branch, then write down that it is dead. The second half is the part people skip." :math "The equality test doubles as a tripwire: it fails the day the constant stops being constant." :cs "Portable code that degrades to a no-op is fine; portable code that degrades to a no-op silently is how the same bug ships twice."))
)
